**Lemma 4.2 (finite PSD completion from the null cone).** If a real
symmetric $R$ is nonnegative on $z^TQz=0$, then $R+\nu Q\succeq0$
for some finite real $\nu$. If it is strictly positive on the unit
null set, a completion can be positive definite.

Proof. The convex cone $\mathcal D=\mathrm{PSD}+\operatorname{span}(Q)$
is closed. Indeed, if $R_n=P_n+t_nQ$ converges with $P_n\succeq0$,
test $P_n$ on one fixed $Q$-positive and one fixed $Q$-negative vector.
These bound $t_n$ above and below. A convergent subsequence of $t_n$
then gives a PSD limit $R-tQ$. If $R\notin\mathcal D$, finite-dimensional
separation gives $X\succeq0$, $\operatorname{tr}(QX)=0$ and
$\operatorname{tr}(RX)<0$. The rank-supported real-null decomposition
in Proposition 2.2 contradicts this last inequality. Thus $R\in\mathcal D$,
which proves the completion with the sign of $\nu$ adjusted. For the
strict assertion compactness of the unit null set gives $e>0$ such that
$R-eI$ is nonnegative there. Complete $R-eI$ and add $eI$. $\square$

